Dutty Boukman</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dutty Boukman was a leader of the Haitian Revolution, born to a Muslim family in Senegambia in about 1767 and enslaved first in Jamaica and then in Saint-Domingue.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dutugemunu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dutugemunu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dutugemunu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dutugemunu was a Sinhalese king of the Anuradhapura Kingdom who reigned from about 161 to 137 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/duwa"><span class="enc-name">Duwa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duwa was a khan of the Chagatai Khanate in the late thirteenth and early fourteenth centuries.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/duy-tan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/duy-tan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Duy Tân</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duy Tân was emperor of Vietnam of the Nguyễn dynasty from 1907 to 1916.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dvaita-vedanta"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dvaita-vedanta.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dvaita Vedanta</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Dvaita Vedanta is a dualist school of Hindu philosophy founded by Madhvacharya in the 13th century in coastal Karnataka.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dvin"><span class="enc-name">Dvin</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dvin was an ancient city of Armenia on the Ararat plain, south of modern Yerevan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dvoryanstvo"><span class="enc-name">Dvoryanstvo</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The dvoryanstvo was the hereditary nobility of the Russian Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dwarasamudra"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dwarasamudra.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dwarasamudra</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dwarasamudra, modern Halebidu in Karnataka, was the capital of the Hoysala kingdom in southern India.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dwight-d-eisenhower.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dwight D. Eisenhower</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dwight D. Eisenhower was a US general and President of the United States from 1953 to 1961.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dyah-pitaloka"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dyah-pitaloka.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dyah Pitaloka</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dyah Pitaloka was a princess of the Sunda Kingdom, daughter of King Linggabuana.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dying-gaul"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dying-gaul.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dying Gaul</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dying Gaul is an ancient marble statue of a wounded Celtic warrior, now in the Capitoline Museums in Rome.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dyle"><span class="enc-name">Dyle</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dyle (Dijle in Dutch) is a river in central Belgium, rising in Walloon Brabant and flowing north through Leuven and Mechelen to join the Nete and form the Rupel.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dynamis-bosporan-queen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dynamis-bosporan-queen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dynamis (Bosporan queen)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dynamis was queen of the Bosporan Kingdom in the late first century BCE, a daughter of Pharnaces II and granddaughter of Mithridates VI of Pontus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dynamite"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dynamite.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dynamite</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Dynamite is an explosive made of nitroglycerin, an absorbent such as clay or diatomaceous earth, and stabilisers, invented in 1866 by the Swedish chemist Alfred Nobel…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dynatoi"><span class="enc-name">Dynatoi</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The dynatoi, &quot;the powerful&quot;, were in Byzantine law the senior ranks of civil, military and church officialdom, usually also holding considerable fortunes and landed…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dzaoudzi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dzaoudzi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dzaoudzi</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dzaoudzi is a town on Petite-Terre, a small island off the east coast of Mayotte in the Comoros archipelago.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dzata"><span class="enc-name">Dzata</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dzata is the site of the stone-walled capital of the Venda kingdom in the Nzhelele valley of Limpopo province, South Africa, traditionally founded by the Singo dynasty…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dzhengutai"><span class="enc-name">Dzhengutai</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dzhengutai is a village in the Buynaksky district of Dagestan, in Russia's North Caucasus, in the foothills between the Kumyk plain and the Avar mountains.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dzibanche"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dzibanche.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dzibanche</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dzibanche is a Maya archaeological site in southern Quintana Roo, Mexico, occupied from the late Preclassic period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dzud"><span class="enc-name">Dzud</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Dzud is a Mongolian term for a catastrophic winter, when heavy snow, ice or extreme cold prevents livestock from grazing and causes mass deaths of animals.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dzungar-genocide"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dzungar-genocide.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dzungar genocide</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dzungar genocide was the mass killing of the Dzungar Oirat Mongols by Qing forces in 1755–1757 under the Qianlong Emperor.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dzungaria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dzungaria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dzungaria</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dzungaria is a region of northern Xinjiang in north-western China, between the Altai and Tianshan mountains.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dzungars"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dzungars.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dzungars</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dzungars were a confederation of Oirat Mongol tribes who formed the last great nomadic empire of Inner Asia in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dzungar-qing-wars"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dzungar-qing-wars.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dzungar–Qing Wars</span><span class="enc-type">War · 1687–1757</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dzungar–Qing Wars were a series of conflicts from 1687 to 1757 between the Qing dynasty of China and the Dzungar Khanate of the western Mongols.</span></a></li></ul>
